      "id": 1,
      "text": "Thurston was originally called Bush City and was laid out by early settlers in 1881.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thurston%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1869 the Atlantic and Lake Erie Railroad extended to the area, and the depot was called Bush Station, making Thurston a railroad town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.thurstonohio.com/history",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A shirt‑waist factory operated in the early 1890s east of the former Harley Hiles property, but it burned down in 1905, visible for miles.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.thurstonohio.com/history",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town’s post office has been in operation since 1881, keeping the community connected for over a century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thurston%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The first school was a two‑room brick building east of the tracks, later turned into a dwelling and destroyed by a tornado in 1953.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
